[E0440] Error: `inner` is `own<Plain>`, which cannot be cloned
    ╭─[ err/406_a_box_implements_nothing/main.wip:19:5 ]
    │
 17 │ @derive(Clone)
    │         ──┬──
    │           ╰──── `@derive(Clone)` writes the clone field by field
    │
 19 │     inner: own<Plain>
    │     ────────┬────────
    │             ╰────────── not a field that clones
    │
    │ Help 1: `own<Plain>` implements `Clone` where `Plain` does, and `Plain` does not
    │
    │ Help 2: write `@derive(Clone)` on `Plain` too, or an `extend` block that implements `Clone` for it
    │
    │ Note: a field clones when its type implements `Clone`: plain data, which owns no memory, is its own clone, and so are a `String`, a `Vec`, an `Option` or a tuple of what clones, and a type that asks for `@derive(Clone)` or writes `extend …: Clone`
────╯
[E0342] Error: `own<Point>` does not implement `Eq`
    ╭─[ err/406_a_box_implements_nothing/main.wip:34:20 ]
    │
 27 │ fn same<T: Eq>(a: &T, b: &T): bool = a == b
    │         ┬
    │         ╰── `T: Eq` declared here
    │
 34 │     io::println("\(same(&a, &b))")
    │                    ──────┬─────
    │                          ╰─────── `T` requires `Eq`
    │
    │ Note: an `own<T>` implements what is written for a box, `extend own<T>`, and not what its `T` does; where `Point` is wanted, `&` of the box lends it, and a type argument written out, `<Point>`, asks for it
────╯
